Make derivation the domain ontology's first explicit relationship

Dev's decisions, 2026-10-09, one point at a time: derivation is the first relationship
the ontology carries explicitly; the edge carries PROVENANCE, with declared edges first
and inferred derivation (classification — a type carrying every element of another, as
Dev observed in ontologies and asked about) as a later rule; the increment is the
relationship alone, the Editor untouched.

draco.Derivation: child, parent, declared (default true). DomainOntology PROJECTS
`derivations` at composition — one edge per named parent of every composed domain's own
definition and members, through DracoAspect.parents, never re-authored, so an edge
cannot drift from the aspect it comes from — and answers owns(domain, typeName),
defines(typeName) (now keys.exists(owns)), dependencies(domain) and including(domain).
Base depends on Draco through Cardinal deriving Primal: the first inter-domain
relationship the ontology can state. The edge keeps the parent AS AUTHORED, parameters
and all (Cardinal(T) derives Primal(T)), so relationships are read by name and package.

DerivationResolvable now reads the edges: a Problem when an edge whose child is the
member names a parent the ontology does not define. Consequence, documented: a member
fact the ontology does not compose has no edges and is not judged. The Editor's
provisional ontology includes the candidate; FoundationValidationTest and
DerivationResolvableRulesTest now compose their danglers in through `including`. A
declared-but-unsupplied member is a placeholder its domain owns — Completeness reports
it once; the edge into it does not report again.

